aybe not the  end but add a little bit more i think  one of the things that's been kind of  underlined repeatedly throughout the  conversation is how many roles there are  and how the growth of the field no one  really knows where it's going to go and  this level of excitement around quantum  computing that i think now is the time  if you're interested in the quantum  computing to go learn to go explore to  go apply to see what you can do see how  you can build your skills into something  that will be useful and contribute to  the ongoing effort i think it's a really  exciting time so i think this  my advice would be just go for it and  try and scale yourself up and all the  ways that everyone said already but just  you know get to work and start  yeah if i might tag on to that james  you know quantum is different quantum is  weird and the only way you're really  going to get it is if you just play with  it and so i encourage everybody to just  experiment you know hop on  bracket for example and use the  different back ends  try to come up with  better ways of solving problems because  a lot of the innovation often happens at  the intersection of different  disciplines and so having somebody come  in that is not an expert in quantum but  just starts experimenting might unlock  some of new solutions applications etc  etc that you know we  physicists or quantum researchers  would not even have thought of just  because we're looking at everything  always from the same angle and so i  think that you know go play roll up the  sleeves hand some keyboards and get it  going  interesting so having  a an experimental approach  uh to  learning quantum computing that's that's  what you're saying  yeah  yeah  i mean it's it's if i just may add to  this the last thing you know it's like  driving a car you can't read a book and  sit behind the wheel  you gotta practice and so it's the same  thing here you know sit down start  coding up  interact with these devices and see what  you can do sorry for interrupting you  but no no what i learned is what  learning is uh  quantum computing is a very  interdisciplinary area  and  it's very important specifically because  of this interdisciplinarity that people  that  maybe have nothing to do with quantum  computing itself get engaged  and can contribute to the field itself  because they can bring a new perspective  maybe that people are  working in the field do not necessarily  have  yeah so well thank you  thanks to my colleagues for joining and  for this uh for this very thoughtful  discussion  for those watching make sure to check  out our website  amazon.science to learn more about  quantum computing and other areas of  customer obsession science at amazon and  bye now  you
 

